Use flag-based symbol visibility for the Kandelo wasm32 target.

For every non-Windows target other than Darwin, FreeBSD and OpenBSD,
Mozilla hides symbols by force-including config/gcc_hidden.h, which opens
`#pragma GCC visibility push(hidden)` around each translation unit, and
re-exposes only the system headers listed in config/system-headers.mozbuild
through generated wrappers. Standard library headers outside that list are
parsed under the hidden pragma.

That scheme does not hold with libc++ 23. Its <__system_error/error_category.h>
forward-declares `class error_condition;` without an annotation, while
<__system_error/error_condition.h> defines it with
_LIBCPP_EXPORTED_FROM_ABI (default visibility). Under the pragma the forward
declaration becomes hidden, so any unwrapped include of <system_error> --
for example from fmt/format.h via mfbt/DbgMacro.h -- fails with "visibility
does not match previous declaration". On Darwin, FreeBSD and OpenBSD, whose
C++ runtime is libc++, Mozilla already uses -fvisibility=hidden instead
(the pragma clash with libc++ headers is discussed in Mozilla bug 1259537).
The flag sets a default for declarations without an annotation, so the
explicit annotation on the definition is consistent with it.

Use that same scheme for wasm32. Visibility is moot for the static Kandelo
wasm executable, which has no dynamic symbol table, so this does not change
what the shell exports.

--- a/build/moz.configure/toolchain.configure
+++ b/build/moz.configure/toolchain.configure
@@ -2220,7 +2220,10 @@
 @depends(target, build_environment)
 def visibility_flags(target, env):
     if target.os != "WINNT":
-        if target.kernel in ("Darwin", "FreeBSD", "OpenBSD"):
+        # Kandelo: wasm32 builds against libc++, like the BSDs, and produces a
+        # static executable with no dynamic symbol table, so use the flag-based
+        # scheme rather than the gcc_hidden.h pragma and system wrappers.
+        if target.kernel in ("Darwin", "FreeBSD", "OpenBSD") or target.cpu == "wasm32":
             return ("-fvisibility=hidden", "-fvisibility-inlines-hidden")
         return (
             "-I%s/system_wrappers" % os.path.join(env.dist),
